✅ 1. == (Reference Comparison)
Compares object references (memory addresses), not actual content.

Returns true only if both references point to the exact same object.

🔍 Example:
java
Copy
Edit
String a = new String("hello");
String b = new String("hello");

System.out.println(a == b); // false — different objects
✅ 2. .equals() (Content Comparison)
Defined in the Object class, and overridden by many classes (like String, List, etc.).

Compares actual content (values) of objects.

🔍 Example:
java
Copy
Edit
System.out.println(a.equals(b)); // true — both strings contain "hello"
⚠️ If not overridden, .equals() behaves like ==.

✅ 3. .compareTo() (Sorting / Comparison)
Comes from the Comparable interface.

Returns:

0 → if objects are equal

< 0 → if the calling object is less than the argument

> 0 → if the calling object is greater

Used for sorting and ordering.

🔍 Example:
java
Copy
Edit
String a = "apple";
String b = "banana";

System.out.println(a.compareTo(b)); // negative (a < b)
🧠 Summary Table
| Operation      | Type               | Purpose                                  | Return Type       |
| -------------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------- | ----------------- |
| `==`           | Reference check    | Checks if both objects are same instance | `boolean`         |
| `.equals()`    | Value check        | Compares object content                  | `boolean`         |
| `.compareTo()` | Sorting/comparison | Orders objects (Comparable)              | `int` (0, <0, >0) |


✅ When to Use What?
| Use Case                                         | Use This       |
| ------------------------------------------------ | -------------- |
| Check if two references point to the same object | `==`           |
| Check if two objects have the same content       | `.equals()`    |
| Sort or compare two values for ordering          | `.compareTo()` |
